ver a plugin path in a form the sweep does not police and never reads bin/prawduct-hook.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py:1085 test_the_blast_radius_check_has_one_home"}], "fid": "R-3"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 3, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Writing the candidate directly reopens a validate-then-rename race under two concurrent derivations, contrary to the new comment's 'a second staging layer would buy nothing'", "recommendation": "derive_branch_cache now writes a fixed-name candidate with `write_text` (which truncates in place), validates it, then calls `os.replace` on it. If a second derivation in the same checkout runs at the same time (ingest in two sessions, which is the threat model tests/test_atomic_state_writes.py cites for every .prawduct state write), it can truncate the inode the first writer just validated. The first writer then renames that inode into `.critic-findings.json` while it is still being rewritten, so readers in that window see torn JSON. The second writer then validates a path that no longer exists and reports `invalid` (ingest prints 'cache was not updated'; the verify dispatch refuses) even though its record is the one that landed. The old atomic_write_text path shared a fixed temp name too, so this is not a clean regression, and it is rare. A per-writer unique staging name keeps the TEMP_SUFFIX coverage and makes the validated inode private to its writer: `tempfile.mkstemp(dir=..., prefix='.critic-findings.json.derived', suffix=TEMP_SUFFIX)`, as backlog/snapshot.py already does. That would also make the comment true.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "test_the_sweep_reaches_the_renames_it_polices re-detects os.replace itself instead of asserting _staged_renames examined those sites", "recommendation": "Its non-vacuity control re-implements the `os.replace` match rather than counti